# How do I create a model in Laravel?

When you first encounter the default `User` model in an older version of Laravel, it can feel like reading documentation for a system that no longer exists. The class implements specific interfaces and uses traits related to authentication (`UserInterface`, `RemindableInterface`) and password hashing (`UserTrait`). If you are building a new project or working with modern Laravel, that boilerplate is largely obsolete.

To create a model for a table like `products` without any authentication logic, you do not need to implement those interfaces or use those specific traits. In fact, adding them would only introduce confusion and unnecessary dependencies on the legacy `Illuminate\Auth` namespace. The core principle of a Laravel model is simplicity: it is an Eloquent class that maps directly to your database table.

You can generate a fresh model using the artisan command:

```bash
php artisan make:model Product -mcr
```

The `-mcr` flag tells Laravel to create the Model, Migration, and Controller for you. However, focusing strictly on the model, here is what you should expect when you inspect the generated file:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Product extends Model
{
    // The table name is inferred from the class name (snake_case)
    // so you don't need to define $table unless your table is named 'products' specifically.
}
```

Notice the absence of `implements UserInterface` or `use RemindableTrait`. This is intentional. In modern Laravel, authentication concerns are handled by the `Auth` facade and specific guard configurations, not by forcing every model to implement a user interface. If your table is named `products`, the `$table` property defaults to `'products'` automatically because Laravel follows the convention of converting the class name from camelCase to snake_case.

You only need to manually set the `$table` property if you are violating that convention, for example, if your database table is actually named `items`.

```php
class Item extends Model
{
    protected $table = 'items'; // Explicitly setting the non-conventional name
}
```

Regarding JSON serialization, older tutorials often included a `$hidden` property to exclude sensitive data like passwords. While this was critical for the legacy `User` model where passwords were stored in plain text or hashed differently, modern Laravel handles this via the `$fillable` and `$appends` properties, as well as accessors for sensitive fields. For a standard product model, you typically leave these empty unless you have specific serialization requirements.

```php
class Product extends Model
{
    // Define which mass-assignable attributes are allowed
    protected $fillable = ['name', 'price', 'stock_quantity'];
}
```

The decision to extend `Model` (or `Eloquent` in legacy contexts) is the only inheritance requirement. This provides you with database querying capabilities, relationship definitions, and lifecycle hooks like `created`, `updated`, and `deleted`.

If you are using a recent version of Laravel, you might also consider adding `$guarded = []` to your model if you strictly want to allow mass assignment for every attribute defined in your migration. This is a trade-off; by default, Laravel guards against mass assignment vulnerabilities by requiring the `$fillable` property. For most applications, explicitly defining `protected $fillable` remains the safer and more explicit approach.

For detailed guidance on setting up authentication features correctly without relying on legacy interfaces, you can refer to the official resources provided by https://laravelcompany.com to ensure your security configuration aligns with current best practices.
